A||||13||burdell1||THIRD BRAKE LIGHT
Z||000000||burdell1||05-28-99||07:04 AM||||I recently purchased a used rear brake light for my 85 GT and was wondering how to install it.  Does anyone out there have any of the wiring diagrams?<br>
Z||000001||PaUL||05-28-99||04:29 PM||||My 86SC came with the 3rd brake light, I think this was the first year for this feature. Dont think earlier models have the needed wiring for this.
Z||000002||burdell1||05-28-99||06:32 PM||||I have all of the wiring (from the donor Fiero), but I am not sure where to put it.<br>
Z||000003||PaUL||05-28-99||08:06 PM||||I think the Haynes manual shows the wiring schematic for this. You could just splice into the brake light wire.
Z||000004||burdell1||05-29-99||01:11 AM||||Where is the brake wire within the car?<br>
Z||000005||Carrolles||06-01-99||10:02 AM||||86 was the first year with third brake lights.
Z||000006||GREENDANO||06-03-99||04:20 PM||||The white wire coming off of brake switch and going towards the steering column is the "brake" circuit.  Only one problem, third brake light will flash with your four-way flashers.<br>  I just added a third brake light to my 85 GT, although I was also adding PISA's new taillights too so I had to rewire a lot.  <br>  Also, the fit was a little funny to the headliner.  Must be the 86 and up cars have a different roof dimple because my light hangs down a bit.  Still, it looks pretty cool having an 85 GT Fastback with a third brakelight.<br>  Dan-O
Z||000007||theogre||06-03-99||06:10 PM||||properly wired, I don't think it blinks with the hazards.......<p>yes, there is a diferant headliner for 86+ cars.
Z||000008||EMF||06-03-99||07:16 PM||||im mounting my soon to be installed  third brake light, on the bottom, right above the  glove box,  on that fuzzyboard  that covers half the area behind the seats.  seems like it would be easiier then  say, cuttin the headliner, and stuff.
Z||000009||GREENDANO||06-22-99||08:22 PM||||Ogre,<br> Properly wiring a pre-86 third brake light entails taping into the white brake output wire going from the brake switch to the dirrectional switch and running the output to the third brake light.  It will flash with your four-ways because Pre 86's don't have the extra circuit that the factory equiped cars had to isolate the third brake light on it's own circuit. <br>  Dan-O 
Z||000010||theogre||06-22-99||09:39 PM||||i'll buy that for a dollar.<p>Over it's short life, Fiero did a huge amount of changing. Much more than I think I've ever seen any other car do. There are enough changes from year to year that it's nearly imposible to track all of them. In just the motor there are at least a dozen from 84-88, and I'm just talking the 4 cyl.<p>Seems to me there should be a place to tap w/o the hazard circuit screwing with it. Maybe on the turn signal harness?<p>If it only blinks with the hazard tho, that's probably a good thing in reality. Yet another blinking light when you are stuck on the side of the road a bad thing it is not. Especially as low as these cars are. The high mount may be the only one that is seen before it gets crunched. ((((Never Ever leave a stranded car anywhere near Philadelphia, PA. I can about guarantee that it will be smashed, intentionally, by the local trash. At the very least, you will need new glass.))))
Z||000011||DJRice||06-24-99||10:47 AM||||It would seem to me that if your not going to mount the brake light on the roof, but on the firewall area instead, that you could just run a wire from one of the brakelight wires themselves around the engine compartment and through the firewall directly to your extra light.......<p>Or you could even install a second switch on your brake pedal and not worry about tapping into the existing wiring....<p>
Z||000012||GREENDANO||06-24-99||04:22 PM||||Ogre,<br>  Fieros never break down so that you have to pull over to the side of the rode,  watch your mouth!!  <br>  Knock on wood.<br>  Dan-O
